EX2=[heading('Example 2'),paragraph('Perform two regula-falsi iterations to approximate the root of f(x)=sin x−e^(−x), with x in radians, on [0.5,0.7].'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('Reset the interval to [a₀,b₀]=[0.5,0.7]. The source values are:'),eq(r'f(a_0)=f(0.5)=-0.127<0,\quad f(b_0)=f(0.7)=+0.148>0'),paragraph('Opposite signs show the root lies in the interval. Equation (i) is used to obtain:'),eq(r'c_0=a_0-\frac{(a_0-b_0)f(a_0)}{f(a_0)-f(b_0)}=0.5-\frac{(0.5-0.7)(-0.127)}{-0.127-0.148}=0.592364'),eq(r'f(c_0)=f(0.592364)=\sin(0.592364)-e^{-0.592364}=+0.081'),paragraph('For a₀=0.5,b₀=0.7,c₀=0.592364, the source lists f(a₀)=−0.127,f(b₀)=+0.148,f(c₀)=+0.081. Retain [0.5,0.592364], discard 0.7, and set [a₁,b₁]=[0.5,0.592364]. Next use equation (i):'),eq(r'c_1=a_1-\frac{(a_1-b_1)f(a_1)}{f(a_1)-f(b_1)}=0.5-\frac{(0.5-0.592364)(-0.127)}{-0.127+0.081}=0.755'),eq(r'f(c_1)=f(0.755)=\sin(0.755)-e^{-0.755}=0.215'),paragraph('The source lists f(0.5)=−0.127,f(0.592364)=+0.081,f(0.755)=+0.215, then claims [0.5,0.755] is the retained interval, discards b₁=0.592364 and sets [a₂,b₂]=[0.5,0.755]. After two iterations c₁=0.755 is declared the approximate root and f(0.755)=0.215.'),heading('Remember'),paragraph('Proceed with regula-falsi only if f is continuous and the endpoint values have opposite signs.'),note('The exact initial endpoint values give c₀≈0.5925284091 and f(c₀)≈0.0055327114. Even the printed first c₀=0.592364 gives f≈0.0053054075, not 0.081. The next denominator must subtract f(b₁), not add the positive value. The exact second iterate is c₁≈0.5886687790 with f≈0.0001886563, and the retained bracket is [0.5,0.5886687790]. A true false-position chord intersection lies inside the current bracket: 0.755 is invalid and expands the source bracket. The published values and errors remain visible as source text, separated from the computed learning answer.')]
def newton():
 def pt(x,y):return(92+64*x,267-27*y)
 f=lambda x:x*x-2
 paths=[path([pt(-.6,0),pt(4.7,0)]),path([pt(0,-2.5),pt(0,7.5)]),path([pt(i/80,f(i/80)) for i in range(225)],stroke='#0369a1')]
 x0=2.5;x1=x0-f(x0)/(2*x0);x2=x1-f(x1)/(2*x1)
 labels=[(401,275,'x'),(82,45,'y'),(300,43,'y=f(x)'),(pt(2**.5,0)[0]-11,293,'r')]
 points=[]
 for x,nextx,name in [(x0,x1,'x₀'),(x1,x2,'x₁')]:
  xx,yy=pt(x,f(x));nxx,nyy=pt(nextx,0);paths.append(path([(nxx,nyy),pt(x+.25,f(x)+2*x*.25)],stroke='#b91c1c'));paths.append(path([(xx,yy),pt(x,0)],dash=True));labels.extend([(xx+7,yy-8,'('+name+',f'+name[-1]+')'),(xx-5,286,name)]);points.append({'x':xx,'y':yy})
 xx,yy=pt(x2,0);labels.append((xx-7,312,'x₂'))
 return geometry('Figure 12.3','Figure 12.3 — schematic Newton tangent intersections x₀ to x₁ to x₂ approaching a root',paths,labels,points=points,size=(440,350))
NEWTON=[heading('(c) Newton-Raphson-method'),paragraph('Newton-Raphson is an iterative numerical procedure and a one-point method requiring one previous approximation, unlike bracketing methods which require two. It uses tangent slopes to the graph of f(x) to approximate roots of f(x)=0. If f and f′ are continuous near actual roots, a sequence of iterates {xₙ} will converge faster than bisection and regula-falsi.'),newton(),paragraph('Let r be a root and x₀ an initial approximation. The tangent at (x₀,f₀) intersects the x-axis at (x₁,0), making x₁ the first approximation. Its slope gives:'),eq(r'\tan\theta=\frac{f(x_0)}{x_0-x_1},\quad f′(x_0)=\frac{f(x_0)}{x_0-x_1}'),eq(r'x_0-x_1=\frac{f(x_0)}{f′(x_0)}\quad\Rightarrow\quad x_1=x_0-\frac{f(x_0)}{f′(x_0)}','i'),paragraph('Similarly the tangent at (x₁,f₁) gives the second approximation:'),eq(r'\tan\theta_1=\frac{f(x_1)}{x_1-x_2},\quad f′(x_1)=\frac{f(x_1)}{x_1-x_2}'),eq(r'x_1-x_2=\frac{f(x_1)}{f′(x_1)}\quad\Rightarrow\quad x_2=x_1-\frac{f(x_1)}{f′(x_1)}'),paragraph('Continue to the (i+1)th iterate:'),eq(r'x_{i+1}=x_i-\frac{f(x_i)}{f′(x_i)},\quad i=0,1,2,\ldots','ii'),paragraph('This is called the Newton-Raphson method.'),heading('Remember'),paragraph('With initial iterate x₀, the iteration xᵢ₊₁=xᵢ−f(xᵢ)/f′(xᵢ) develops a sequence converging faster than bisection and regula-falsi.'),note('Require f′(xᵢ)≠0 at every computed step and preserve the function’s real domain. Continuity of f,f′ alone does not guarantee convergence or faster convergence: an unsuitable start can diverge, cycle, hit a zero derivative or leave the domain. With suitable smoothness near a simple root and a sufficiently close start, convergence is locally quadratic; multiple roots may yield only linear convergence. A bracket gives safeguards that unrestricted Newton iteration does not. The native figure is schematic tangent geometry, not the specified polynomial of the following example.')]
